1
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Fight like a viking, fight to survive

2
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:58,000
The Lost Viking is a action, adventure, history film directed by Emmet Cummins.

3
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:24,000
A young Viking called Vitharr arrives in Britain with dreams of conquest and adventure, but when his family and clan are ambushed
and killed, he must survive alone in this strange,dangerous new land as he searches for his Uncle's settlement.

4
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:50,000
Vitharr, played by Dean Ridge.

5
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Wyman, played by Ross O'Hennessy.

6
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Herja, played by Kezia Burrows.

7
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:08,000
The Hermit, played by Ioan Hefin.

8
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:34,000
Asbjorn, played by Ian Virgo.

9
00:30:54,000 --> 00:31:00,000
Cassius Dominus, played by James Groom.

10
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:26,000
Atlantis: The Lost Empire is a 2001 American animated action adventure film directed by Gary
Trousdale and Kirk Wise, produced by Don Hahn, and written by Tab Murphy.

11
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:52,000
Produced by Walt Disney Feature Animation, it stars Michael J.

12
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:18,000
Fox, James Garner, Cree Summer, Don Novello, Phil Morris, Claudia Christian, Jacqueline Obradors,
Florence Stanley, David Ogden Stiers, John Mahoney, Jim Varney, Corey Burton and Leonard Nimoy.

13
00:44:38,000 --> 00:44:44,000
Set in 1914, it follows young linguist Milo Thatch, who gains possession of a sacred book, which
he believes will guide him and a crew of mercenaries to the lost city of Atlantis.

14
00:48:04,000 --> 00:48:10,000
Development of the film began after production had
finished on The Hunchback of Notre Dame (1996).

15
00:51:30,000 --> 00:51:36,000
Instead of another musical, directors Trousdale and Wise, producer Hahn, and screenwriter
Murphy decided to do an adventure film inspired by the works of Jules Verne.

16
00:54:56,000 --> 00:55:02,000
Atlantis: The Lost Empire was notable for adopting the distinctive visual style of
comic book artist Mike Mignola, one of the film's production designers.

17
00:58:22,000 --> 00:58:28,000
The film made greater use of computer-generated imagery (CGI) than any of Disney's previous
traditionally animated features and remains one of the few to have been shot in anamorphic format.

18
01:01:48,000 --> 01:01:54,000
Linguist Marc Okrand constructed an Atlantean
language specifically for use in the film.

19
01:05:14,000 --> 01:05:20,000
James Newton Howard provided the film's musical score.

20
01:08:40,000 --> 01:08:46,000
The film was released at a time when audience interest in animated films was
shifting away from traditional animation toward films with full CGI.

21
01:12:06,000 --> 01:12:12,000
Atlantis: The Lost Empire premiered at the El Capitan Theatre in Hollywood, Los
Angeles, on June 3, 2001, and went into its general release on June 15.

22
01:15:32,000 --> 01:15:38,000
The film received mixed reviews from critics.

23
01:18:58,000 --> 01:19:04,000
Budgeted at around $90–120 million, Atlantis grossed over $186 million worldwide, $84 million of which was earned in North America; its lackluster box office
response was identified as a result of being released in competition with Shrek, Lara Croft: Tomb Raider, The Fast and the Furious and Dr.

24
01:22:24,000 --> 01:22:30,000
Dolittle 2.

25
01:25:50,000 --> 01:25:56,000
As a result of the film's box office failure, Disney cancelled a planned spin-off animated television series, Team
Atlantis; an underwater Disneyland attraction; and a volcanic Magic Kingdom attraction based on it.

26
01:29:16,000 --> 01:29:22,000
Atlantis was nominated for several awards, including seven Annie Awards,
and won Best Sound Editing at the 2002 Golden Reel Awards.

27
01:32:42,000 --> 01:32:48,000
The film was released on VHS and DVD on January 29,
2002, and on Blu-ray on June 11, 2013.

28
01:36:08,000 --> 01:36:14,000
Despite its initial reception, reception in later years became favorable and has given Atlantis a cult following and
reappraisal from critics as a mistreated classic, due in part to Mignola's unique artistic influence.

29
01:39:34,000 --> 01:39:40,000
A direct-to-video sequel, Atlantis: Milo's Return, was released in 2003.
